HIKE=L('Crowsnest Pass "Take a Hike" trail sheet (2023)','https://crowsnest.albertacf.com/images/2023/Take_a_Hike-back_2023.pdf')
MUSEUM=L('Crowsnest Museum: visit','https://crowsnestmuseum.ca/visit')
COLNHS=HP('Coleman National Historic Site of Canada',11933)
FMPHA=L('Town of Fort Macleod: Provincial Historic Area','https://www.fortmacleod.com/play/historic-downtown/historic-downtown')
PLACES=[
# ---- The east end of the Pass: Passburg, Bellevue, Hillcrest, Frank ----
N('leitch-collieries','Leitch Collieries (Passburg)','Crowsnest Pass','ab','industry','ruins',49.55923,-114.32107,'site','public','walk','','1907–1916',
  'The only fully Canadian-owned coal company in the Crowsnest Pass, opened in 1907 on Police Flats, where the North West Mounted Police had kept a post against cattle rustlers from 1883 to 1901. It built 101 coke ovens, a wooden washery and a tipple, and a sandstone power house that lit the townsite of Passburg from 1910. The coal turned out poor for coking and only a few ovens were ever fired. The company failed in 1916, after the 1911 general strike and the loss of markets in the war, and most of Passburg\'s buildings were moved to Bellevue.',
  'Gravelled self-guided paths (about 0.8 km, roughly an hour) through the shell of the power house in bluish-grey sandstone quarried on the site, with concrete sills and arches and an "AD 1920" inscription on the south gable; the roofless sandstone walls of the manager\'s house in a gully; the concrete footings of the tipple and washery; the weigh-scale pits; and the long mound of the coke-oven bank with its loading wharf, where a short stretch of sandstone rubble still shows oven doorways and brick linings. A sandstone quarry is cut into the hill above the east end of the ovens.',
  'Canada\'s own coal company, beaten by bad coal, a strike and a war.',
  'A Government of Alberta provincial historic site (designated 1983), north of Highway 3 between Burmis and Bellevue. Open May 15 to Labour Day, self-guided, admission by donation; outside that season the washrooms are closed and there is no winter maintenance. Large parking lot, open May 15 to Labour Day, no overnight parking. Dogs on a leash on the paths; there is an off-leash area. Group bookings through the Frank Slide Interpretive Centre.',
  [W('Passburg, Alberta'),HP('Leitch (Passburg) Collieries',8746),LEITCH,HIKE],th=[49.55866,-114.32127],thn='Leitch Collieries lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?'),
N('bellevue-underground-mine','Bellevue Underground Mine (West Canadian Collieries)','Bellevue','ab','industry','standing',49.57793,-114.36537,'exact','public','walk','','1903–1961',
  'West Canadian Collieries, founded in 1903 by the French businessmen Jules Justin Fleutot and Charles Remy, worked mines at Lille, Bellevue and Blairmore; by August 1905 it had 150 miners at Bellevue. On December 9, 1910 a falling rock set off an explosion here that killed 30 miners and one rescuer, Alberta\'s worst industrial disaster until Hillcrest four years later. The mine closed in 1961 and its tipple, which straddled rail spurs where Highway 3 now runs, was taken down. It was designated a Provincial Historic Resource in 2011.',
  'The paired concrete portals, with the company name and construction date cast into the face, beside a remnant of the tipple walkway; old mine machinery round the yard and artifacts in the main building. Guided hour-long tours go about 1,000 feet into the main haulage tunnel, 150 feet below the surface, with helmet and lamp; the mine stays around freezing.',
  'Walk into the mine where 31 men died in 1910.',
  'Owned and run by the Crowsnest Pass Ecomuseum Trust Society. Guided tours only, booked ahead (walk-ins not guaranteed): from the May long weekend to the end of August, hourly 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. on weekdays and every half hour on July and August weekends; in September daily tours from 11 a.m., the last at 3:30; in October evening "dark history" tours only; closed in winter. Dress warmly. Groups of eight or more must book. Fees on its page.',
  [L('Bellevue Underground Mine: hours and rates','https://www.bellevuemine.com/visit/tour-information'),L('Bellevue Underground Mine','https://www.bellevuemine.com/visit'),L('RETROactive: Coal mining history and heritage conservation at the Bellevue Mine (2024)','https://albertashistoricplaces.com/2024/07/17/coal-mining-history-and-heritage-conservation-at-the-bellevue-mine/')],th=[49.57786,-114.36566],thn='Mine lot on 20 Avenue (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?'),
N('hillcrest-cemetery-ab','Old Hillcrest Cemetery (1914 mine disaster graves)','Hillcrest','ab','burial','standing',49.57619,-114.38081,'site','view','walk','cemeteries','1914',
  'On the morning of June 19, 1914 an explosion in the Hillcrest Collieries mine, thought one of the safest in the Pass, killed 189 of the men underground, most of them by the afterdamp gas left when the fire used up the air. It is reckoned Canada\'s worst mining disaster. Of the 237 who went in (the Register says 235), 48 were brought out alive. 180 of the dead were buried in mass graves in this cemetery on the eastern slope of Turtle Mountain.',
  'The rows of the miners\' graves and their markers, laid out together, inside a perimeter fence of steel cable, wire and rail with concrete steps or a stile at the south entrance.',
  'The mass graves of Canada\'s worst mine disaster.',
  'Owner and visiting hours not checked (the Municipality of Crowsnest Pass cemeteries page answered 404). A Provincial Historic Resource. Parking on 223 Street to the southeast (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('Hillcrest mine disaster'),HP('Old Hillcrest Cemetery',8747)],th=[49.57505,-114.37948],thn='Lot on 223 Street (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?'),
N('frank-slide','Frank Slide and the buried end of Frank','Frank','ab','town','traces',49.59111,-114.39528,'area','public','walk','','1901–1903',
  'At 4:10 a.m. on April 29, 1903 the east face of Turtle Mountain broke away and in about a hundred seconds slid some four kilometres across the Crowsnest valley, burying the southern end of the coal town of Frank, the Canadian Pacific line and the mine plant. Between 70 and 90 people died, most still under the rock; seventeen miners dug themselves out after 13 hours. The railway was rebuilt in three weeks and the mine reopened, but the part of town nearest the mountain was moved in 1911 and the mine closed for good in 1917.',
  'A boulder field of limestone covering about 508 hectares, left nearly as it fell (alteration is barred), crossed by Highway 3 and a 1.5 km trail through the rocks from the interpretive centre. In the old townsite, now the Frank Industrial Park, the Register lists depressions where buildings stood, the old street lines and one cast-iron fire hydrant. The interpretive centre has galleries and a 30-minute film.',
  'A mountain fell on a town at 4 a.m.; most of the dead are still under it.',
  'A provincial historic site. The Frank Slide Interpretive Centre (Government of Alberta) is 1.5 km off Highway 3: open all year 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., daily from mid-May to mid-October and closed Mondays the rest of the year, closed Christmas Eve, Christmas Day, New Year\'s Day and Easter Sunday. Adult $18; fees on its page. The trail and viewpoints are free to walk from its lot; dogs on a leash outside, not in the building. A second lot, the Frank Slide Viewpoint, is on 20 Avenue.',
  [W('Frank Slide'),W('Frank, Alberta'),HP('Frank Slide',11500),FRANK,HIKE],th=[49.60092,-114.39387],thn='Frank Slide Interpretive Centre lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('lime-city-kilns-frank','Lime City kilns','Frank','ab','industry','ruins',49.5868,-114.38294,'area','view','walk','','1910–1923',
  'The slide gave a free supply of limestone. A Guelph, Ontario buyer took part of it in 1909 and built two concrete draw kilns the next year; the Winnipeg Fuel and Supply Company bought the works in 1912 and added a third kiln of rubble stone, and ran them until 1923. Boulders were broken up, hoisted to the top and burned with wood; the lime was drawn out at the bottom and shipped in barrels made on the spot. A manager\'s house and bunkhouses round the kilns were known as Lime City.',
  'Three tall masonry kilns, rectangular and tapering to the top: the two eastern ones of board-formed concrete, the western one of rubble stone, with brick-lined draw doorways at their south foot, a concrete loading-platform wall with traces of limewash, and the embankment of the rail spur.',
  'Selling the rockslide back by the barrel.',
  'Part of the Frank Slide provincial historic site; who owns the kiln ground and whether there is a path to them was not checked. The nearest mapped parking is the Frank Slide Viewpoint lot on 20 Avenue, about 750 m northwest.',
  [HP('Frank Slide',11500),L('Waymarking: Lime City, Frank, AB','https://www.waymarking.com/waymarks/WMPE0M_Lime_City_Frank_AB')],conf='mixed',th=[49.59132,-114.38718],thn='Frank Slide Viewpoint lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?'),
N('lille-ab','Lille ghost town and its Bernard coke ovens','Crowsnest Pass','ab','town','ruins',49.6521,-114.397,'area','ask','hike','','1901–1914',
  'Prospectors for a French company found coal on Gold Creek in 1901 and set up "French Camp". By 1903 West Canadian Collieries had three mines here and a spur of the Frank and Grassy Mountain Railway, and the camp was renamed after Lille in France, where its backers were. About 400 people lived in some 80 buildings, with waterworks, electricity, a hotel, a hospital and a four-room school. Fifty Bernard coke ovens, assembled and tested in Belgium and then rebuilt here in 1904, made coke for the smelters. The mines closed in 1912; by 1914 the town was empty and its buildings pulled down or moved.',
  'In a meadow at the meeting of Gold and Morin creeks: the ruins of the Bernard coke ovens beside the slack-coal piles (Wikipedia says the assembly numbers are still on many bricks), the concrete foundation of the two-storey hotel on a bench above Gold Creek, a couple of fire hydrants, and many cellar hollows along the old streets. Take nothing: it is a Provincial Historic Resource.',
  'A French company town rebuilt brick by numbered brick from Belgium.',
  'A 6.3 km walk each way on rough roads with free-range cattle, two crossings of Gold Creek on footbridges and fords of Green and Morin creeks (Gold Creek is swift and icy early in the season). The published route starts north of the Frank Slide Interpretive Centre road: cross the cattleguard at the hairpin and park in the meadow on the second rough road to the left, or park at the centre. The route is in the Crowsnest Pass trail sheet and the Friends of the Frank Slide Centre\'s hiking guide, but who owns the land was not checked, so treat it as ask-first until it is.',
  [W('Lille, Alberta'),HP('Village of Lille',11391),HIKE,L('Hiking the Southern Rockies: Ghost Town of Lille (Friends of the Frank Slide Centre)','https://research.uleth.ca/spire/documents/pdf/Ghost_Town_of_Lille.pdf')],th=[49.60626,-114.39157],thn='Meadow parking off the Frank Slide centre road (trail sheet)',brick='?'),
# ---- Blairmore and Coleman ----
N('blairmore-courthouse','Blairmore Courthouse','Blairmore','ab','civic','standing',49.60731,-114.43151,'exact','view','drive','','1923',
  'Built in 1922 and 1923 when Prohibition had brought bootlegging and smuggling across the B.C. line to a Pass already troubled by train robberies and strikes. It was the first courthouse in Alberta to hold police as well as the court: offices and barracks for the Alberta Provincial Police and cells for men and women. R. P. Blakey of the provincial Department of Public Works designed it in a Spanish Colonial Revival style borrowed from the missions of the American southwest.',
  'A two-storey stucco-covered masonry and clay-tile building with brick inlays, a low hipped roof with curved gables and exposed rafter ends, and stepped arched parapets, at the east end of the main street. The Register names the floor plan, staircase, courtroom and interior trim among the surviving fabric; whether the public can go in was not checked (interior not checked).',
  'The courthouse built to stop the rum-runners.',
  'A Provincial Historic Resource. Current owner and use not checked; see it from 20 Avenue.',
  [W('Blairmore, Alberta'),HP('Blairmore Courthouse',2891)],brick='?'),
N('coleman-main-street','Coleman: the old company town and its main street','Coleman','ab','town','standing',49.63405,-114.50215,'area','public','walk','','1903–1983',
  'The International Coal and Coke Company laid out Coleman in 1903 beside its mine. It became one of the main coal towns of the Pass, the greatest coalfield in Alberta and southeastern B.C., through strikes (1911 and 1932), floods and the 1948 fire that took the opera house. Underground mining stopped about 1960 and the surface plant worked coal from open pits until 1983. It was made a national historic site in 2001 as the only place in the region where a mining community survives next to its mine works.',
  'Along 17 Avenue, a main street of one- to three-storey brick, wood and stucco fronts set flush with the sidewalk, facing the railway and the mine side; on 18 Avenue, the street north of it, the old high school (now the Crowsnest Museum), the Alberta Provincial Police barracks, the bank, the fire hall and the company\'s offices; and the 1905 miners\' union hospital, later the union hall, a T-shaped frame building with an enclosed veranda. West Coleman, cut off by a rock outcrop, is rows of small wooden miners\' cottages on narrow lots. The Register also names the miners\' path from the houses on the height to the mines.',
  'A coal company\'s whole town, still standing beside its pit.',
  'Public streets of the Municipality of Crowsnest Pass, just off Highway 3; free street parking downtown (Crowsnest Museum). The buildings are private or in use; see them from the street.',
  [W('Coleman, Alberta'),COLNHS,HP('Coleman Union Hall / Hospital',11844),MUSEUM],brick='?'),
N('crowsnest-museum-coleman-high','Crowsnest Museum (old Coleman High School)','Coleman','ab','civic','standing',49.63486,-114.5026,'exact','public','drive','','1936–1937',
  'Coleman\'s high school classes had been taught in Blairmore or in rented rooms until the school board built its own central high school in 1936 and 1937, to the province\'s standard plan. Unlike the other high schools in the Pass it had an auditorium, used for night classes in English for the town\'s many immigrants. It closed as a school in 1963 and now holds the Crowsnest Museum.',
  'A plain two-storey red-brick school with decorative brickwork over the upper windows, a stone string course and coping, and a central entrance tower. The Register names the original staircase, floors, ceilings, the auditorium and stage, doors, light fixtures and trim among the surviving interior. The museum is inside.',
  'A Depression-era school with its stage, now the Pass\'s museum.',
  'Crowsnest Museum, 7701 18 Avenue. Open 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; winter hours from November 1, Monday to Friday 10 to 4. Adult $10, which includes the A.P.P. Barracks; fees on its page. Free street parking downtown. Wheelchair accessible.',
  [HP('Old Coleman High School',3007),MUSEUM,COLNHS],brick='yes'),
N('app-barracks-coleman','Alberta Provincial Police Barracks','Coleman','ab','civic','standing',49.63482,-114.50136,'exact','public','drive','','1904',
  'Built in 1904 as two joined company cottages, typical of the International Coal and Coke Company\'s miners\' houses. It housed the North West Mounted Police briefly, a town constable from 1910, and from 1918 two constables of the Alberta Provincial Police, who shared it with the town police until the force was disbanded in 1932. On September 21, 1922 APP Corporal Stephen Lawson was shot dead in front of it; the bootlegger Emilio Picariello and Florence Lassandro were hanged for it, Lassandro the only woman ever hanged in Alberta.',
  'A small hip-roofed wooden bungalow with drop siding and two corbelled brick chimneys. Inside, the Register lists the floor plan, fir flooring, wainscoting in the front office and marks of the old cast-iron stoves. Open as part of the Crowsnest Museum.',
  'Where a bootlegger shot a policeman in 1922.',
  'Run by the Crowsnest Museum (admission to the museum includes the barracks); same hours as the museum. Next door to the 1905 union hospital.',
  [HP('Alberta Provincial Police Building',2890),MUSEUM],brick='?'),
N('crowsnest-pass-polish-hall','Crowsnest Pass Polish Hall','Coleman','ab','civic','standing',49.63189,-114.49699,'exact','view','drive','','1927',
  'The Polish Society of Brotherly Aid was founded in the Pass in 1916 to keep miners\' families from ruin after an accident; it grew into a centre for a Polish school, a library, a choir, an orchestra, a drama group and a hockey team. With more than 240 members by 1927, nearly half of them in Coleman, the society built this hall, much of it by volunteer labour.',
  'A single-storey brick hall covered in stucco, with a stepped parapet bearing the year 1927 in relief, carved lumps of coal on either side of the parapet, brick pilasters and a projecting entrance porch. The Register mentions folk-art murals inside; interior not checked.',
  'A miners\' mutual-aid hall with coal carved on its front.',
  'A Provincial Historic Resource at 1406 82 Street. Owner and opening not checked; see it from the street.',
  [HP('Crowsnest Pass Polish Hall',15493)],brick='no'),
N('coleman-coke-ovens','International Coal and Coke Company coke ovens','Coleman','ab','industry','ruins',49.63264,-114.50389,'site','view','walk','','1903–1952',
  'The International Coal and Coke Company began 100 beehive coke ovens in 1903 as part of its mine and townsite, and added 116 more in 1906, for about 8,000 tons of coke a month, most of it for the smelters of southern B.C. Coal cars on rails along the top emptied into the ovens. They closed in 1918 when metal prices fell, 104 were relit in 1932, and the last ten went cold in 1952.',
  'A long row of the brick ovens in a continuous wall along the railway at the south edge of town, part of the national historic site\'s mine side with the old machine shop, wash house and power plant. The Register of 2009 also lists the 1971 steel processing plant; whether it still stands was not checked.',
  'Two hundred ovens that baked coal for fifty years.',
  'Owner and any path to the ovens not checked. They lie beside the Canadian Pacific line, which runs between them and the town: do not walk on the tracks. Free street parking downtown, north of the tracks (Crowsnest Museum); the mapped lot on 76 Street is a school lot.',
  [COLNHS,W('Coleman, Alberta'),L('Waymarking: Coleman Coke Ovens (interpretive sign text)','https://www.waymarking.com/waymarks/WMPCCJ_Coleman_Coke_Ovens_Coleman_AB')],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
# ---- East of the Pass: Lundbreck and Fort Macleod ----
N('doukhobor-prayer-home-lundbreck','Doukhobor Prayer Home','Lundbreck','ab','civic','standing',49.5841,-114.16269,'area','view','drive','','1954',
  'Doukhobors, pacifist dissenters from the Russian Orthodox Church helped out of Russia by Tolstoy and others, reached Saskatchewan in 1899; many later moved to British Columbia, and the first Alberta colonies were founded round Cowley and Lundbreck in 1915. This prayer home was built in 1954, with a kitchen added behind in 1983. It is one of the few Doukhobor buildings left in Alberta.',
  'A plain one-storey building in asbestos shingles under a metal gable roof, on three lots in the hamlet. Interior not checked.',
  'One of the last Doukhobor meeting houses in Alberta.',
  'At 753 Kettles Street in the hamlet of Lundbreck (Register). Owner and opening not checked; see it from the street.',
  [HP('Doukhobor Prayer Home',16362)],brick='no'),
N('territorial-court-house-fort-macleod','Territorial Court House (old Fort Macleod Town Hall)','Fort Macleod','ab','civic','standing',49.72458,-113.40737,'exact','view','drive','','1902–1904',
  'Built in 1902 to 1904 by the federal government for the Alberta Judicial District of the North-West Territories, to a standard plan by the Dominion architect David Ewart. It is the oldest court house in Alberta and the only territorial court house that went on working under the provincial courts, until 1971. It was then the town office until 2018, when the town moved out and put it up for sale; it was a national historic site from 1980 and had parts in the film Brokeback Mountain and the series Fargo.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey red-brick building trimmed with bands of rusticated sandstone on a stone foundation, under a cross-gabled roof, with arched windows, blind arches, returned eaves and a porch over the central steps. The Register says some original interior finishes survive; the 2018 sale notice mentioned three old cells in the basement. Whether the public can go in now was not checked.',
  'Alberta\'s oldest court house, sold off by its town.',
  'In the Fort Macleod Provincial Historic Area downtown. Who owns it since the 2018 sale was not checked; see it from the street. Town lots nearby on 23 and 25 Street.',
  [HP('Territorial Court House NHS',12566),HP('Fort Macleod Courthouse (Town Hall)',5917),FMPHA,L('CBC News: Southern Alberta courthouse made famous in Brokeback Mountain for sale (2018)','https://amp.cbc.ca/lite/story/1.4739294')],brick='yes'),
N('empress-theatre-fort-macleod','Empress Theatre','Fort Macleod','ab','amuse','standing',49.72562,-113.40741,'exact','public','drive','','1910–1912',
  'Announced in the Lethbridge Herald in January 1910 as a "first class" opera house and finished in 1912 by the contractor J. S. Lambert for the owner T. B. Martin, in the boom years on Fort Macleod\'s Main Street. It served as a vaudeville house, concert and lecture hall and live theatre and later as a cinema, and was richly refurnished in the early 1940s. It reopened in November 2025 after a summer of renovation.',
  'A two-storey red-brick front (Wikipedia photograph) of locally made brick with corner piers, a curved parapet, a large arched entrance, an oculus lighting the projection room and a projecting marquee. Inside, the Register and Wikipedia name the original balcony, seating, dressing rooms and projection booth; performers have signed the basement dressing-room walls since at least 1923. Open to ticket holders for shows; tours not checked.',
  'A 1912 opera house where vaudeville acts signed the walls.',
  'At 235 24 Street, on the Main Street of the Fort Macleod Provincial Historic Area. A non-profit theatre; show times on its own site. Town lot on 25 Street.',
  [W('Empress Theatre (Fort Macleod)'),HP('Empress Theatre',5891),L('Town of Fort Macleod: Empress Theater','https://fortmacleod.com/play/tourism/empress-theater2'),FMPHA,L('Global News: Historic southern Alberta theatre reopens (2025)','https://globalnews.ca/news/11515610/historic-southern-alberta-theatre-reopens')],th=[49.726,-113.40733],thn='Lot on 25 Street (OpenStreetMap)',brick='yes'),
]
